#!/usr/bin/env bash

COMMIT_TYPE=${CLAUDE_COMMIT_TYPE:-"conventional"} # One of: conventional, default
COMMITS_TO_SUGGEST=${CLAUDE_COMMITS_TO_SUGGEST:-5}
LOCALE=${CLAUDE_LOCALE:-"en-US"}
MAX_LENGTH=${CLAUDE_MAX_LENGTH:-72}

# Check for staged changes
diff=$(git diff --cached | head -n 10)
if [ -z "$diff" ]; then
  echo "No changes in staging. Add changes first."
  exit 1
fi

COMMIT_TYPE_DETAILS=""
# Conventional commit types and descriptions
if [ "$COMMIT_TYPE" = "conventional" ]; then
  COMMIT_TYPE_DETAILS="
   These are the conventional commit types you can use, never use any other type.

   <conventional_commit_types>
   - feat: A new feature
   - fix: A bug fix
   - docs: Documentation only changes
   - style: Changes that don't affect code meaning (formatting, etc)
   - refactor: Code changes that neither fix bugs nor add features
   - perf: Performance improvements
   - test: Adding or correcting tests
   - build: Changes to build system or dependencies
   - ci: Changes to CI configuration
   - chore: Other changes that don't modify src or test files
   - revert: Reverts a previous commit
   
   <format>type(scope): description</format>
   <example>feat(auth): add user login functionality</example>
   <example>fix(api): resolve null pointer exception in user service</example>
   
   </conventional_commit_types>
"
fi

pnpm dlx @anthropic-ai/claude-code "
You are a helpful assistant specializing in writing clear and informative Git commit messages using the ${COMMIT_TYPE} style
Based on the given code changes or context, generate exactly ${COMMITS_TO_SUGGEST} ${COMMIT_TYPE} Git commit message based on the following guidelines.
1. Message Language: ${LOCALE}
2. Format: follow the ${COMMIT_TYPE} Commits format: ${COMMIT_TYPE_DETAILS}
4. Guidelines for writing commit messages:
   - Be specific about what changes were made
   - Use imperative mood (\"add feature\" not \"added feature\")
   - Keep subject line under ${MAX_LENGTH} characters
   - Do not end the subject line with a period
   - Use the body to explain what and why vs. how
5. Focus on:
   - What problem this commit solves
   - Why this change was necessary
   - Any important technical details
6. Exclude anything unnecessary such as translation or implementation details.

Only return the commit message(s) in your response. Do not include any additional meta-commentary.
Generate $COMMITS_TO_SUGGEST different commit suggestions.

IMPORTANT: Do not wrap your response in markdown code blocks. Do not use \`\`\` in your response at all.
Just list each commit message directly, one per line.

Here is an some info on current state of the repository:

<git_status>
$(git status)
</git_status>

<git_log>
$(git log --oneline -10)
</git_log>

Here is the diff:

<git_diff>
$(git diff --staged)
</git_diff>

Try to be as fast as possible, try not to use additional tools, since the info provided should suffice.
"
